# Numeric (double and mixed precision)

## Double on the stack

A **double** is two cells; **MS cell on TOS**. Document `( d -- )` / `( d1 d2 -- d3 )` in headers.

```forth
123 s>d              \ signed n → d
456 0                \ unsigned u → ud (push 0 on TOS)
d1 d2 d+ d>s         \ double add, narrow to single if in range
```

Input: `3465.` and `3.465` are the same double; `1.` without `e` is **not** a float — see `forth-floating-point.mdc`.

## Mixed single/double

Use when intermediate product would overflow a cell:

```forth
1000 3 */            \ (1000*3)/1 in double precision
n1 n2 m* d.          \ full-width product (signed: m*, unsigned: um*)
d1 n m+              \ add single into double
d1 n1 fm/mod         \ floored div/mod of double by single
```

Pick division semantics explicitly: `fm/mod` (floored) vs `sm/rem` (symmetric remainder).

## Pictured numeric output

Build strings **right-to-left** in the hold area: `<# … #>`. Operates on **unsigned double** — promote singles:

```forth
: u. ( u -- )
  0 <<# #s #> type space #>> ;

: . ( n -- )
  dup abs swap
  s>d swap over dabs
  <<# #s rot sign #> type space #>> ;
```

Gforth nested holds: `<<# … #>>`; reset with `<#` if the hold area is exhausted. **`.r` is formatted output**, not return-stack debug — see `forth-debugging.mdc`.

## Base and conversion

Keep `base` decimal in application code; save/restore around `hex`/`oct` sections. Prefer `base-execute` over manual `base @ >r … r> base !`. Use `$`/`#`/`%` prefixes in source for non-decimal literals when the manual documents them.
